WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.253
- Five, four, three, two, rehearsal.

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:10.520
- Business Insider.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:12.385
Oh, this sounds exciting.

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:17.860
- In 1969 our founders, Joan
Cooney and Lloyd Morrisett

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:21.620
had the revolutionary idea of asking,

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.214
what if kids can learn from TV?

00:00:24.214 --> 00:00:28.635
They had noticed that kids
could repeat beer jingles.

00:00:28.635 --> 00:00:30.570
♪ Where there's life there's Bud ♪

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:31.684
- What'll you have?

00:00:31.684 --> 00:00:33.260
♪ Pabst Blue Ribbon ♪

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:34.850
- So they posed themselves that question

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:37.319
and basically created Sesame Street

00:00:37.319 --> 00:00:41.080
which is a show that's now been
on the air almost 50 years.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:42.910
We film at Kaufman Astoria Studios

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:44.410
which is in Astoria, Queens

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:46.510
and is a very old studio

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:47.760
that was built in the '20s

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.700
by what was United Artists at the time

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.810
and has gone through many
iterations over the years.

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:54.760
On a regular production day, like today,

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:55.840
when you're visiting,

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:57.960
there's about 60 people involved

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:59.560
when you count the production staff

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:01.250
that's downstairs in the office

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:04.810
as well as all of the
technical and stage crew

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:08.310
and camera people and
costume designers, et cetera.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:11.130
It really does take a village
to bring this show together.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.690
Every year when we start a new season

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:15.790
what we do is our curriculum team

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:17.950
convenes an all-day seminar.

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:19.870
And they basically consult with experts

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:24.200
in child development, child
psychology, preschool teachers.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:26.460
As the writers work the curriculum team

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:28.690
works in tandem with the production team

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:31.810
and they review all the
scripts and give feedback

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:34.191
from the educational standpoint.

00:01:34.191 --> 00:01:36.550
We also do a lot of research.

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:39.400
So we'll take some of
the scripts into schools,

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:40.780
we'll read them to the kids

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:43.410
and solicit feedback from them.

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:46.820
Our production cycle is
essentially a 12-month cycle.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:49.550
You know, from the moment
of that curriculum seminar,

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:50.450
which I described,

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:53.770
to the moment we deliver shows to HBO.

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:56.230
Jim Henson, you know, obviously his impact

00:01:56.230 --> 00:01:58.240
on Sesame Street is massive

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:03.240
and he was the one who brought
The Muppets to Sesame Street.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:07.370
And so his legacy, I mean, is very solid

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:08.600
and lives on on the show

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:11.850
because all of our puppeteers
are trained, basically,

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:14.520
in a methodology that he invented.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:16.950
I mean, he took the puppet theater

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:19.130
and decided, I'm gonna get rid of that

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:23.130
and just use the TV frame
as my puppet theater

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:24.600
while looking at a monitor

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.720
so that I can position my puppet

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:28.640
and see exactly what I need to see.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.890
And so, basically he invented, you know,

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:32.770
puppetry for television.

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:36.320
And our puppeteers are
trained in those methods

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:37.370
that he came up with.

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:39.610
It truly is, like, kind of a magic act.

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:41.950
So we have a core group of puppeteers

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:45.480
that is 14 people, 14 performers.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:47.900
We have a mentee program that we run

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:49.610
to identify new puppeteers.

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:51.670
And so there's a few of those

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:54.010
and we'll bring them in
a day here, a day there.

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:57.960
There's essentially three
categories of puppets.

00:02:57.960 --> 00:02:59.500
There's the walk around puppet.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:01.800
So that's Big Bird or Snuffy, for example,

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.840
where the person is inside the puppet

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.130
and they're operating it from inside.

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:07.570
It's a full suit.

00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:10.160
- You and me together, ol' pal.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:11.970
- Then there's what we call a bag puppet.

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:14.810
So that's, like, Cookie
Monster or The Count.

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:17.080
- Om, nom, nom, nom, nom, nom, nom.

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:19.603
- One, two, three, four.

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:23.450
- And so there the puppet
covers the puppeteer.

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:25.575
The puppeteer's operating
the mouth with one hand

00:03:25.575 --> 00:03:27.810
and is operating one of
the hands with another

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:29.730
and then there's a second puppeteer,

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:32.680
which we usually call
the assistant puppeteer,

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:35.370
and that person comes behind
and operates the other hand

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:37.930
and helps hand props from below frame.

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:41.410
Then third kind of puppet is
what we call a rod puppet.

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:44.240
So that's like an Elmo or an Abby.

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:45.436
And so they have,

00:03:45.436 --> 00:03:48.210
those are typically operated
by a single puppeteer.

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:50.350
They hold two rods which operate the hands

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:52.540
and then they're operating
the mouth this way.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:55.090
The primary puppeteer is always the person

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:56.404
that does the voice.

00:03:56.404 --> 00:03:57.678
- Oh, Cookie Monster,

00:03:57.678 --> 00:04:00.407
how could you mistake
the mushroom for cookies?

00:04:00.407 --> 00:04:03.776
- So, the most recent and
probably most talked about

00:04:03.776 --> 00:04:05.920
for the US is Julia,

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:09.280
who's a young girl who's
on the Autism spectrum.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:11.400
- Can Big Bird see your painting?

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.030
- See your painting, yes.

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:15.950
- And she was developed originally

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:18.380
by our Social Impact team.

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:21.050
Another example on the international side,

00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:22.850
because we do a lot of international work,

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:27.037
is Zari who was developed for Afghansitan.

00:04:27.037 --> 00:04:28.716
- Welcome to the street.

00:04:28.716 --> 00:04:29.959
- Thank you Elmo.

00:04:29.959 --> 00:04:32.097
Today I'm so excited to be here.

00:04:32.097 --> 00:04:32.930
- [Both] Yay!

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:33.763
(both laugh)

00:04:33.763 --> 00:04:34.660
- She's six years old.

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:38.430
She's curious and also
has a younger brother

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:40.570
who looks up to her which
is a great, kind of,

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:42.660
gender equity role modeling.

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:47.100
Caroll Spinney has been here
since season one, you know,

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:51.310
and is the originator of
both Oscar and Big Bird.

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:53.130
He's obviously a legend

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:56.663
and an amazing performer
and just an amazing guy.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:04:59.180
We have a couple of crew members,

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:00.013
believe it or not,

00:05:00.013 --> 00:05:02.093
who've been here for nearly 50 years.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:05.200
Frankie, who's our Camera One operator,

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:07.210
has been here since the beginning.

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:10.650
Our sound effects editor, Dick Maitland,

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.500
has also been here since the beginning.

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:15.100
They work on Sesame Street
because they love Sesame Street.

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:16.570
They believe in our mission.

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:18.220
So people tend to stay.

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:20.840
In a given season of 35 episodes

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:25.658
we'll have, you know, 10 to
12 celebrity appearances.

00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:27.470
We love to do musical guests

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:29.370
because kids love music.

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:32.710
And, you know, the celebrities
are for the parents

00:05:32.710 --> 00:05:35.310
you know, more so than the kids

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:37.610
who may not know who John Legend is

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:40.320
but when we can combine both,

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:42.850
doing musical and a celebrity appearance,

00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:44.480
then it's great for everyone.

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:46.420
Celebrities will reach out to us

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:48.740
especially if they've recently had kids

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:50.220
and they think to themselves,

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:54.210
wow, I would love for
Junior to see me on TV.

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:56.030
Sesame Street has changed a lot

00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:58.070
and our production models evolve

00:05:58.070 --> 00:06:00.610
with new technology all the time.

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:05.610
So we did a big kind of redesign
of the set in season 46.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:07.670
So that was about four years ago.

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:09.630
And it was, you know,

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:13.270
obviously we kept 123, the
brownstone and Hooper's.

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:16.130
Those are the touchstones of the set.

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:18.420
But we wanted to modernize it some

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:22.740
to give it a more colorful
and vibrant atmosphere

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:26.570
and also to position the
puppets in specific places.

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:30.150
So Abby, who is a fairy,
we put her in her garden.

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:33.250
And so now every kid who watches
Sesame Street knows, like,

00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:35.050
that's Abby's garden,
that's where she lives,

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:38.250
that's where she hangs out
and plays with her friends.

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:40.060
There's hundreds of studies

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:42.630
that have shown the
effectiveness of Sesame Street

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:45.050
and how kids who watch Sesame Street

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:47.420
are ready for school in a fashion

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:49.760
that's greater than those who don't.

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:51.470
- Why me love cookies?

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:54.370
Oh, they just the perfect food.

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:57.600
They round, they crunchy,

00:06:57.600 --> 00:06:58.770
they soft in the middle,

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:01.370
they chocolate, mmm, chocolatey.

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:02.780
Sorry, me got to go
get a cookie right now.

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:03.613
Excuse me.

